WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pdp_171'), 'The nightly job, as it ran on the bad night:

```python
def nightly_load(db, export_rows):
    db.executemany("INSERT INTO orders VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)", export_rows)   # dashboards read orders
    db.commit()
    run_checks(db, table="orders")    # alerts on failures
```

```text
02:10  load committed, 20,412 rows
02:11  check delivered_total_positive FAILED (6,180 rows)
08:00  finance dashboard: revenue -30% vs last Thursday
```

Statement: "The check did catch the problem; it could not protect the dashboard because it ran on the published table after the commit. Loading into staging, checking there and publishing only a passing batch (write, audit, publish) keeps the bad night out of the dashboard."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The check failed at 02:11, but the rows had been visible to every dashboard since 02:10. Write-audit-publish loads into a staging table (or a branch / partition not yet exposed), runs the checks there and swaps or inserts into the published table only when the error checks pass. The dashboard then shows yesterday''s complete data plus an alert instead of wrong numbers. The misconception is that a check that alerts is enough; where it runs decides whether anyone is protected.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pdp_171') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
